@page "/exgrid/sorting-filtering"
@using ExGrid.Docs.Examples.Grid

<DocsPage Product="ExGrid" Title="Sorting, filtering and find"
          Lead="The grid never sorts, filters or searches rows itself: it does not hold them. It offers the gestures, and asks whoever holds the rows to answer. GridSource.From answers for a list in memory, and its answers are the reference for what each one means.">

    <DocsSection Title="With GridSource.From">
        <p>
            Bind a <code>GridSource.From(rows)</code> and everything on this page works with no further code:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>Click a header to sort.</strong> One click sorts ascending, a second descending, a third
                returns to your own order. A click replaces the whole sort with that one column; a multi-column
                sort is expressed in code, as a list of <code>SortSpec</code>.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Open the ▾ in a header</strong> (or press <kbd>Alt</kbd>+<kbd>↓</kbd>) for the column menu
                and the filter panel. A column declares which panel it offers with <code>filterUi</code>: a
                <code>ValueList</code> of distinct values to tick, a <code>Condition</code> (the default), or
                <code>Both</code>. The value list is asked for when the panel opens; past 1,000 distinct values it
                gives way to the condition form.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>F</kbd></strong> in the grid for the find panel. It searches
                every row of the result, not only the painted ones, and matches the text each cell shows.
                With several cells selected, it searches the selection. If the rows move, or another
                <code>Source</code> is handed over, while a step is out, its answer moves nothing: the panel says
                to find again, and <code>OnFindRefused</code> says <code>OrderChanged</code> or
                <code>SourceChanged</code>.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <DocsExample Title="Sort, filter and find" Builtin="typeof(SortFilterFind)" Mud="typeof(SortFilterFindMud)"
                     Files="@(["Examples/Data/Trade.cs"])" />
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="What sorting and filtering mean">
        <p>
            <code>GridSource.From</code> is the reference implementation: a server that sorts or filters for the
            grid should answer exactly as it does. The rules are short:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>Text compares <strong>ordinally, ignoring case</strong>, everywhere: the same answer on every machine and in every culture, and one a database can match.</li>
            <li>A <strong>Blank</strong> is a value accessor returning <code>null</code>. An empty string is a value, not a Blank.</li>
            <li><strong>Blanks sort last</strong> in both directions, as in Excel, and the sort is stable.</li>
            <li>A Blank matches only <code>IsBlank</code>, or a value list that ticks it. <code>NotEquals</code> and <code>DoesNotContain</code> do not match a Blank.</li>
            <li>Which operators a column offers follows its type: <code>Contains</code> and <code>StartsWith</code> for text, <code>GreaterThan</code> and friends for numbers and dates.</li>
            <li>Anything ambiguous is <strong>refused with an exception naming the column</strong> — an unknown column, an operator the type does not allow, a value of the wrong type — rather than guessed at.</li>
        </ul>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Sorting and filtering from code">
        <p>
            The sort and filter are plain data — <code>SortSpec</code> and <code>GridFilter</code> — so a toolbar
            button, a saved view or a link can set them. Call the source's <code>OnSortChanged</code> and
            <code>OnFilterChanged</code>; the header's sort arrow and the filter panel follow, and the source raises
            <code>StateChanged</code> whenever the result changes, whoever changed it.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Title="A saved view" Builtin="typeof(QueryFromCode)" Mud="typeof(QueryFromCodeMud)"
                     Files="@(["Examples/Data/Trade.cs"])" />
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="When you hold the rows yourself">
        <p>
            Without a Source you push the rows (see <a href="exgrid/data">Data sources</a>), and the gestures arrive
            as notifications. Pass <code>Sorts</code> and <code>Filters</code> so the header and panel show what is in
            force, answer <code>OnSortChanged</code> and <code>OnFilterChanged</code> with a new <code>Window</code>,
            <code>OnDistinctValuesNeeded</code> for the value list, and <code>OnFind</code> for
            <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>F</kbd>. <code>GridQueryEngine.Apply</code> and <code>GridFind.Step</code> are the
            reference implementations, if you want to run them over rows of your own.
        </p>
        <p>
            Bump <code>RowSequenceVersion</code> whenever the order of the rows changes. The grid holds only the
            Window and cannot see a reorder; the version is how it knows to drop a selection that would now cover
            other rows.
        </p>
        <p>
            Where nothing can answer a find, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>F</kbd> is <strong>refused</strong> rather than
            left to the browser, whose find sees only the rows on screen and would report "no match" for a row a
            little further down.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="API">
        <ApiTable Type="typeof(InMemoryGridSource<>)" />
        <ApiTable Type="typeof(SortSpec)" />
        <ApiTable Type="typeof(GridFilter)" />
        <ApiTable Type="typeof(FilterSpec)" />
        <ApiTable Type="typeof(FilterClause)" />
    </DocsSection>
</DocsPage>
